---
title: Catalogue
render: static
---
<!--
  The public catalogue — prerendered to plain HTML at build time.

  There is no `publishes:` in the frontmatter above, which is a claim: this page
  contains nothing that is not public. The build CHECKS that claim rather than
  taking it — it taps the Litestone client while load() runs and compares every
  model read against that model's @@gate. `Product` is @@gate("0.4.4.5"), so its
  read level is 0 and the page is allowed to exist.

  Point the load() at a gated model and `bun run build:site` fails, naming the
  route, the model and the level. See index.meta.js.

  ── The two islands ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Everything outside the island tags ships as HTML and nothing else. The two
  components below are the only JavaScript on this page, one chunk each, and
  each says WHEN it wants to run:

    CatalogList  client:load      the list itself — prerendered, then live, so
                                  the search box works and a crawler still sees
                                  every product
    LiveStock    client:visible   below the fold: what can actually be ordered
                                  right now, asked of the running API

  `bun run verify:public` drives the BUILT output in a real browser and asserts
  both — a prerendered page that looks right and does nothing is the failure
  this app exists to catch.
-->
<script>
  import CatalogList from '../../islands/CatalogList.mesa'
  import LiveStock   from '../../islands/LiveStock.mesa'

  export let data = null
</script>

<div class="container stack">
  <h1>Catalogue</h1>
  <p class="text-muted">{data?.products?.length ?? 0} products, prerendered.</p>

  <CatalogList client:load products={data?.products ?? []} />
</div>

<!--
  LiveStock is pushed below the fold on purpose: `client:visible` must not fetch
  its chunk until it scrolls into view, and the drive asserts exactly that
  against the browser's resource timing.

  This band is what does the pushing, and it is real content on its own ground
  rather than an empty spacer — it sits OUTSIDE the page container so it can run
  edge to edge, with a container of its own inside. `min-height`, not `height`:
  the floor is all the assertion needs, and a band that grows past it keeps the
  island below the fold either way.
-->
<section class="promise">
  <div class="container stack">
    <h2 class="h4">Shipping and returns</h2>
    <ul class="cards">
      <li class="card">
        <div class="surface-body stack">
          <strong>Free delivery over $75</strong>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            The threshold the checkout actually applies — a row in
            ShippingMethod, not a line of copy.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <div class="surface-body stack">
          <strong>30 days to change your mind</strong>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            A refund is a declared transition on the order, gated so that only a
            supervisor can run it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <div class="surface-body stack">
          <strong>Made in small runs</strong>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Which is why availability below is asked of the shop rather than
            baked into this file.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="container">
  <LiveStock client:visible />
</div>

<style>
  .promise {
    /* The FLOOR the lazy-island assertion needs — whatever this grows to,
       #live-stock starts below the first screen. */
    min-height:    100vh;
    display:       grid;
    align-content: center;
    padding-block: var(--space-6xl);
    background:    var(--surface);
    border-block:  var(--border-width) solid var(--rule);
  }
  .cards {
    list-style: none;
    margin:     0;
    padding:    0;
    display:    grid;
    gap:        var(--space-2xl);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));
  }
</style>
